{
  "dom_id": "step-q320722",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Expressed in radians, $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)=${{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $-1$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the fourth quadrant, where the tangent is negative.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-1.\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is an angle in the fourth quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$",
